task: growing-small-capacity
agent: opus5-bench
started: 2026-09-08
branch: feature/growing-small-capacity
worktree: ../okay-wt-cap

why: the last thing between here and the default. Everything else is
  settled: growth pays 4.1x / 7.9x / 22.8x at two, four and sixteen
  producers and costs 10% at one. The single unexplained fact is that
  `Source.merge`, at `capacity = 64`, is 3.5x SLOWER under a growing
  default, while the same buffer at capacity 1024 is the best lane on
  the page.

  Three hypotheses have been measured and refuted (the part scan, the
  part sizing, a per-element consumer). What is left is the capacity
  itself, and it has never been swept.

what: a small benchmark class of its own — `MergeCapBenchmark` — that
  runs `Source.merge` at capacity 64, 256 and 1024, so the sweep does
  not multiply every lane in MergeBenchmark by three. Then ring
  against growing across all three, alternating, on a quiet box.

predict: the regression shrinks as capacity rises and is gone by 1024,
  because ManyProducers at 1024 shows growth winning at two producers.
  If so the fix is a FLOOR: `Channel.apply` partitions only above some
  capacity, since a buffer too small to split is not worth splitting.
  DISQUALIFYING: if 1024 regresses too, capacity is not the variable
  and the difference is something else about `Channel.merge`'s shape —
  in which case I say so and the default stays blocked rather than
  getting a threshold pulled out of the air.
